{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And she turned aside before me</b> – They explained in it: \"and she turned aside into the field, as she had done before me.\" But there is no need for this; rather its meaning is like \"because of me\" (מִפָּנַי), for thus the lamed serves, as in \"וַיַּחְדְּלוּ לִבְנוֹת הָעִיר\" (Genesis 11:8), \"חָדַל לְהַשְׂכִּיל לְהֵיטִיב\" (Psalms 36:4), and so many others. And similar to it: \"וַיֵּצֵא יוֹאָב וְשָׂרֵי הַחַיִל לִפְנֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ לִפְקֹד אֶת הָעָם אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל\" (II Samuel 24:4)." "And the meaning of <b>surely she turned aside from before me</b> – according to the commentators it is like \"had she not turned aside from before me,\" serving in this instance unlike all its cognates. But in my opinion it is according to its plain sense, for the she-ass sensed the angel or saw him and did not know what the matter was, but turned aside because of a drawn sword, as is the way of animals. Therefore the angel said to Balaam: \"And the she-ass saw me and turned aside from before me,\" and perhaps she turned aside from before me, for I came to kill you, but her I kept alive, for the sin is in you and not in the she-ass." "<b>and you too I would have killed</b> – Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra (on Numbers 22:33) said that the word \"גַּם\" is evidence that the she-ass died after she spoke, and the meaning of \"I kept alive\" is that a human being, upon seeing the angel, would die, and the proof is \"וַתִּנָּצֵל נַפְשִׁי\" (Genesis 32:31), even though a beast that has no partnership with the angel at all, as a human being does. But the word \"גַּם\" is not interpreted as he said, for both of them would not die, for now that she turned aside the she-ass died and he kept Balaam alive, and had she not turned aside he would have killed Balaam and kept the she-ass alive. Rather, the word \"גַּם\" is according to the words of Rashi, that it is like an abbreviation: \"even you I would have killed,\" and the meaning of \"and her I kept alive\" is to say that the entire sin is upon you, and I would have killed you on its account, but she has no sin for which she should die. Now, the turning aside — that she turned aside from before me — was for your benefit, not for her own need at all, and you struck her for nothing and repaid her evil for good. And Scripture did not indicate whether the princes of Balak were with Balaam during these events, or whether they were riding ahead of him and there was a distance between them and him. The more probable is that they were with him and would not separate from him, and they saw the turning aside of the she-ass and Balaam striking her, and they assumed it was just the way of a stubborn beast, and they did not hear her speech — even the word of the angel, which was the opening of Balaam's eyes. And our Sages said (Numbers Rabbah 20:14) that the princes of Moab were guileless, for they saw a sign unlike any other in the world, and they said (there) that once she spoke she died, so that the nations should not say: \"This is the one that spoke,\" and make her into an object of reverence. And furthermore, the Holy One, blessed be He, had regard for the honor of the wicked man, so that they should not say: \"This is the one that undid Balaam.\" And all this is possible by way of received tradition (מִדֶּרֶךְ הַקַּבָּלָה), not that Scripture spoke of her death at all, as I have explained."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The phrase מִסְמָרְסָה / כִּמְסֹרָס is here rendered following Rashi's own gloss on the word גַּם in this verse, meaning a shortened or elliptical form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:13.446324269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}